 # Coverage as a policy
 
-A coverage number is the easiest number in a build report to produce and the easiest one to be wrong
-about. This page is the policy that number is judged against: which places have to be walked, out of
-what, and why the threshold is not a hundred.
+A coverage number is the easiest number in a build report to produce and the easiest one to be
+wrong about. `cargo llvm-cov` over this project answers one percentage about everything in it, and
+that percentage is dominated by the modules nobody argues about: the wiring, the adapters that
+exist so a type can be named — code that has no branches worth a case, and that is genuinely
+covered.
+
+What this page states is the other half: **which places have to be walked, out of what, and why the
+threshold is not a hundred**. The measurement is [`kiln`'s coverage gate] and this page is the claim
+that measurement is judged against, so the two are read together.
 
 The scope is `src/engine`: the rules of this queue, which are what a case can hold a threshold over.
-<!-- TODO: say why the service around them is not in this policy, and what a percentage that averaged
-the two would be a percentage about. -->
+The service around them ([the daemon and the CLI]) is wiring, and a percentage that averaged the two
+would be a percentage about `main`.
 
 ## What the policy is
 
-<!-- TODO: the threshold and the kind of place it counts, as numbers and in the words
-`CoveragePolicy::standard()` uses in `src/engine/coverage.rs` — the spec compares this page with
-that method, so a page that says something else is a page that has stopped being true. Say which
-places make the ceiling below a hundred, and why the two numbers are related. -->
+**90% of the branches of the engine.** A *branch* is one way out of a decision: the arm of a `match`,
+the two sides of an `if`, the `Err` arm of a `?`. A *line* is not the same question, and the
+difference is the whole reason this page exists: an error arm no case ever takes leaves every line
+of that function covered and half of its decisions unexercised, so a suite reporting lines reports
+the half that the compiler already told us about.
+
+Below `src/engine` there are ten branches in the inventory below, and **one of them no case can
+take** (see [What cannot be walked]), so the highest share any run can reach — the *ceiling* of this
+inventory — is **90%**, which is exactly what the policy asks for. That is deliberate and it is
+uncomfortable: `Inventory::ceiling` exists so that `CoveragePolicy::is_satisfiable` can say when a
+threshold has walked past what the code allows, and the next honest exemption anybody adds has to
+come with a lower threshold in the same diff.
 
 ## The inventory
 
+One row per place a case has to walk, and the case that walks it. A row without a case is a wish; a
+`case` without a place is unverifiable.
