 ## What cannot be walked
 
-<!-- TODO: the branches no case can reach, and the reason for each. An entry without a reason is the
-one this section exists to catch, and `Inventory::exempt` takes the reason as an argument. -->
+- `src/engine/clock.rs` — the body of `SystemClock::now`. It reads the wall clock: a case that took
+  this branch would make the suite depend on the machine's time, and every other instant in the
+  crate comes through the injected [`Clock`] for exactly that reason. The place stays in the
+  inventory, because it is a place the module has; the exemption is what says no case can take it.
+
+Adding another exception is a decision somebody has to write down here with its reason, and it is
+the reason `Inventory::exempt` takes one: a page that lists places without saying why is a page
+whose first entry is "this one was annoying".
 
 ## How this is measured and checked
 
-<!-- TODO: the command that measures it, what the gate judges it over, and what it answers on a
-machine without the LLVM tools. Link the pages this one is built on: [the compiler's
-instrumentation](TODO), and [the tool that reads a profile back](TODO). -->
+`kiln check jobq/3.21-coverage-policy --slow` runs the `coverage` gate, which builds the project
+with `-C instrument-coverage`, runs this step's suite under it, and judges the **lines of this step's
+own module** — the same place the `mutants` gate mutates. On a machine without the LLVM tools the
+gate answers `blocked` and prints the install line rather than a number; that is the honest answer
+and it is not a failure of this project.
+
+What the gate cannot check is this page, so the spec does: the engine's policy
+(`CoveragePolicy::standard()`), the module it is about, and the columns of the inventory are
+compared with the page you are reading — a page that states a different threshold is a page that has
+stopped being true.
+
+## Where this comes from
+
+- [`-C instrument-coverage`] — what the compiler emits, and why a profile needs the binaries it came
+  from;
+- [`llvm-cov`] — `export --summary`, which is the document the gate reads;
+- [`grcov`] — the same profiles without the LLVM tools, and a good example of what a coverage report
+  looks like when it is built around files rather than around a single number.
